{ id:'enthd-nose-262', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'*[Back-reference. The book prints only "In the previous case…"; the antecedent (Q260, same page) is a **newborn who looks agitated and cyanosed but becomes less agitated with improvement of the cyanosis upon crying**, diagnosed on **CT** as **bilateral choanal atresia**.]*\n\nIn that case, what is the **first line of management of this infant after confirming the diagnosis**?',
  options:['Surgical intervention','Endotracheal intubation','Conservative management','Hyperbaric oxygen therapy'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Secure the airway first — a neonate with bilateral choanal atresia has no nasal airway at all, and of the four options only intubation gives him one.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` states the physiology that makes this an emergency in two lines: **"Obligate nasal breathing for 6 to 8 WKs (degree and duration is variable)"** and **"Complete nasal obstruction in a newborn may cause death from asphyxia."** That is why the cyanosis lifts when the baby cries — crying forces an oral airway open — and returns the moment he settles or feeds.\n\nThe same deck prints the management ladder for the bilateral case verbatim: **"Bilateral : creating oral airway — 1. Mcgovern nipple, 2. Oral airway, 3. intubation"**, then **"Stimulate infant to cry"**, then **"Genetics consult, work up for other anomalies."** Definitive repair comes afterwards, under **"Surgical Approaches — Transnasal, Transpalatal, Transseptal."**\n\n| Option | Why it is not the first line |\n|---|---|\n| **Surgical intervention** | the **definitive** treatment, and the deck lists it as a separate later step. An unventilated neonate cannot be taken to theatre until an airway exists |\n| **Endotracheal intubation** | **keyed** — the third rung of the deck’s own oral-airway list, and the only airway measure printed among these four options |\n| **Conservative management** | this is what the deck reserves for the **unilateral** case: *"Unilateral: delayed to allow the patient to grow, and reduce risk of restenosis."* Bilateral atresia is not observed |\n| **Hyperbaric oxygen therapy** | no role anywhere in the deck. The lesion is a **mechanical** block, not a diffusion or delivery failure — oxygen at any pressure still cannot pass an atretic choana |\n\n**⚠️ The rung chosen depends on which rungs the paper offers, and the corpus prints both.** Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose1-40` keys **"Intubation and orogastric tube feeding"** for the same question; the endpoint file’s `entep-mfe4-31` keys **"Oropharyngeal airway placement"** because that paper offers the airway and the intubation side by side. Here the McGovern nipple and the oral airway are not printed, so intubation is the only airway on the page and the key is sound.\n\n**Bottom line: airway, then work-up, then repair — and never observation, because the newborn cannot switch to mouth breathing on his own.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose1-40`, `entep-mfe4-31`, `entqb-nose1-36` (neonates are obligate **nasal** breathers), `entep-nose-105` (definitive transnasal endoscopic repair), and `enthd-nose-265` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bilateral choanal atresia in a newborn is an airway emergency: create an oral airway (McGovern nipple, oral airway or intubation) first, and defer definitive choanoplasty until the baby is stable.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.80 (Nose ch.1). **⚠️ Recorded defect: the printed stem reads only "In the previous case", so it cannot stand alone in a shuffled deck — the antecedent Q260 (same page, keyed CT scan) is restated in the stem here.** Obligate nasal breathing, death from asphyxia, the bilateral oral-airway ladder, "Stimulate infant to cry" and the three surgical approaches are transcribed from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`. **Cross-bank note recorded, key not disputed: `entqb-nose1-40` keys intubation and `entep-mfe4-31` keys an oropharyngeal airway — different option sets, same principle.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-264', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **incorrect match** between each meatus and the structures that open into it?',
  options:['Inferior meatus, nasolacrimal ducts','Middle meatus, maxillary sinus','Superior meatus, sphenoid sinus','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The sphenoid sinus does NOT open into the superior meatus — it drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess, and the superior meatus receives the posterior ethmoids.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` prints the whole lateral-wall map on one slide, under the heading *"The Lateral wall (3 turbinates & 3 meati)"*:\n\n| Drainage site | What opens there (slide’s own words) |\n|---|---|\n| **Inferior meatus** | *"NLD opens in the inferior meatus"* |\n| **Middle meatus** | *"Frontal, anterior ethmoids & maxillary sinuses opens in the middle meatus"* |\n| **Superior meatus** | *"Posterio ethmoids opens in the superior meatus"* |\n| **Sphenoethmoidal recess** | *"Sphenoid sinus opens in the sphenoethmoidal recess"* |\n\nThe recess is not a meatus at all — it is the cleft **above and behind the superior turbinate**, between that turbinate and the nasal septum. Calling it the superior meatus collapses two separate drainage sites into one, which is exactly the error the question is built on.\n\n**Why the other options are correct matches:** the **nasolacrimal duct** into the inferior meatus is the anatomical reason tears run into the nose when you cry and the reason the inferior meatus is the route for a dacryocystorhinostomy; the **maxillary sinus** into the middle meatus is one third of the osteomeatal complex, together with the frontal and the anterior ethmoids, and it is why `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` puts *"Pus in the middle meatus"* on its endoscopic-findings slide. **"None of the above"** fails because option C is a real mismatch.\n\n**Clinically this is a localising sign.** The same deck pairs pus in the middle meatus with the anterior group (frontal, anterior ethmoid, maxillary) and **"Pus in the sphenoethmoidal recess"** with the posterior group — so where the pus is tells you which sinuses are infected.\n\n**Bottom line: three meati, but four drainage sites — the sphenoid needs the recess, not a meatus.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-45` and `entqb-nose1-45` (both turn on the sphenoethmoidal recess), `entqb-nose1-4`, `entep-nose-21`, `entqb-nose1-6`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The nasolacrimal duct drains into the inferior meatus, the frontal/anterior ethmoid/maxillary sinuses into the middle meatus and the posterior ethmoids into the superior meatus — but the sphenoid drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess, not a meatus.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.80 (Nose ch.1). The four-line lateral-wall drainage map is transcribed verbatim from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; *"Pus in the middle meatus"* and *"Pus in the sphenoethmoidal recess"* are from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`. The position of the recess relative to the superior turbinate and septum, and the dacryocystorhinostomy note, are added and are not on the slide.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-265', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'A **37-week gestation baby born by caesarian section** developed **cyanosis soon after birth** and needed resuscitation. Oxygen saturation improved but he **soon developed cyanosis again** and was transferred to the neonatal ICU. It was noted that the **cyanosis disappears when the child cries**. **Respiration was not noisy** and clinical examination revealed a **left parasternal pansystolic murmur suggestive of VSD**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Respiratory distress syndrome','Cyanotic heart disease','Laryngomalacia','Bilateral choanal atresia'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Cyanosis that disappears when the baby cries is the single pathognomonic sign of bilateral choanal atresia — and the murmur is an associated anomaly, not the diagnosis.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` prints the sign in the deck’s own words: **"Bilateral choanal atresia: Symptomes of severe airway obstruction and cyclical cyanosis alleviated by crying are the classic signs of neonatal bilateral atresia."** The physiology sits two slides earlier — **"Obligate nasal breathing for 6 to 8 WKs"** and **"Complete nasal obstruction in a newborn may cause death from asphyxia."** A newborn cannot voluntarily mouth-breathe; crying is the one manoeuvre that opens the oral route, so every cry pinks him up and every settled interval re-cyanoses him. That is the cycle the stem describes.\n\n**The murmur is a trap, and the deck itself explains why it is there.** Choanal atresia is *"Commonly associated with other craniofacial anomalies"*, the deck’s named example being the **CHARGE association**: *"C coloboma of the eye, **H congenital heart disease**, A atresia of the choanae, R retarded growth and development, G genital anomalies in males, E ear abnormalities and deafness."* A VSD in this baby is the **H** of CHARGE sitting alongside the **A** — supporting evidence for the airway diagnosis, not a competing one.\n\n| Option | Why it fails on this stem |\n|---|---|\n| **Respiratory distress syndrome** | surfactant deficiency of the **preterm**; this baby is 37 weeks. It causes **constant** distress with grunting, retractions and tachypnoea that **worsens** with effort — it does not remit with crying |\n| **Cyanotic heart disease** | the pattern is inverted. A right-to-left shunt cyanoses **more** on crying (the hypercyanotic spell), and cyanosis is not relieved by improving ventilation. A **pansystolic** murmur of a VSD is in any case the murmur of an **acyanotic** left-to-right shunt |\n| **Laryngomalacia** | supraglottic collapse gives **inspiratory stridor**, and the stem states explicitly that **"respiration was not noisy"**. It also presents over weeks, not in the delivery room |\n| **Bilateral choanal atresia** | **keyed** — cyclical cyanosis relieved by crying, silent breathing, immediate onset, associated cardiac anomaly |\n\n**Two features are doing the work, and both are negatives worth naming.** *Not noisy* removes every laryngeal cause; *relieved by crying* removes every cardiac and parenchymal cause, because those are the ones effort makes worse. `L23` calls choanal atresia **"the most common congenital nasal anomaly (1 in 7,000 to 1 in 10,000 live births)"**, and the diagnosis is confirmed on **"Investigations — 1. C.T"**.\n\n**Bottom line: pink when crying, blue when quiet, and quiet when blue — that is bilateral choanal atresia until CT says otherwise.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose1-43` (cyanosis relieved by crying), `entqb-nose1-42`, `entqb-nose1-36`, `entqb-nose1-37` (the CHARGE associations), `entqb-nose1-38`, and `enthd-nose-262` / `enthd-nose-269` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Cyclical neonatal cyanosis relieved by crying, with silent breathing, is bilateral choanal atresia — and an accompanying VSD is the congenital heart disease of the CHARGE association, not an alternative diagnosis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.81 (Nose ch.1). **Printed typo kept as transcribed: the stem reads "caesarian section"; the correct spelling is caesarean — noted once and not corrected in the stem.** The classic-signs line, obligate nasal breathing, the incidence figure, the CHARGE list and "Investigations — C.T" are transcribed from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`. **⚠️ Recorded as a paediatric-cardiology vignette printed inside a nose chapter; chaptered by content to `ent-noseanat`.** The behaviour of RDS, hypercyanotic spells and laryngomalacia used to exclude the distractors is added *(not taken from the course material)* — `L6) Stridor and tracheostomy.pdf` covers laryngomalacia as a stridor cause but the comparison itself is written here.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-266', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'**Perpendicular plate of ethmoid bone** shares in the formation of …… While **cribriform plate of ethmoid bone** shares in the formation of ……?',
  options:['Medial nasal wall, Nasal floor','Medial nasal wall, Nasal roof','Lateral nasal wall, Nasal floor','Lateral nasal wall, Nasal roof'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**One bone contributes to two different walls: the perpendicular plate drops down as the bony septum — the medial wall — while the cribriform plate lies flat as the roof.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` prints both halves on separate slides. For the roof and floor: **"Roof — Cribriform plate, Frontal bone anteriorly"** against **"Floor — Palatine process of maxilla, horizontal process of palatine bone"**. For the septum: **"Anatomy of the nasal septum — Boney septum: perpendicular plate of ethmoids, Vomer bone. Cartilagenous septum: septal cartilage."** The septum **is** the medial wall of each nasal cavity, since the deck opens by describing the nose as *"Divided by the nasal septum into two nearly symmetrical cavities"* and labels the walls *"Lateral wall & medial wall"*.\n\n| Structure | Which wall | Slide |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Perpendicular plate of ethmoid** | **medial** (bony septum), with the vomer below and behind it | septum slide |\n| **Cribriform plate of ethmoid** | **roof**, with the frontal bone in front | roof/floor slide |\n| **Palatine process of maxilla + horizontal plate of palatine** | **floor** (the hard palate) | roof/floor slide |\n| **Turbinates, meati, uncinate** | **lateral** wall | lateral-wall slide |\n\n**Why the wrong options are wrong:** anything pairing the perpendicular plate with the **lateral** wall (C and D) confuses it with the ethmoid’s **other** contribution — the ethmoidal labyrinth and the superior and middle turbinates, which really are lateral-wall structures, but are not the perpendicular plate. Anything pairing the cribriform plate with the **floor** (A and C) puts the olfactory nerves under the nose instead of over it.\n\n**Clinical weight of the roof.** The cribriform plate is perforated by the olfactory filaments and is the **thinnest barrier between the nose and the anterior cranial fossa**; that is why a fracture there gives CSF rhinorrhoea and anosmia, and why `L23` places *"The olfactory area: The upper part of the nose related to the superior turbinate, cribriform plate"* at the roof.\n\n**Bottom line: ethmoid down the middle is septum; ethmoid across the top is roof.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-22` (the cribriform plate is the one bone **not** in the septum) and `enthd-nose-279` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The perpendicular plate of the ethmoid forms part of the bony nasal septum (medial wall) while the cribriform plate forms the nasal roof.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.81 (Nose ch.1). The roof, floor, medial/lateral wall labels, the bony and cartilaginous septum list and the olfactory-area slide are transcribed from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`. The CSF-rhinorrhoea consequence of a cribriform fracture is added *(not taken from the course material)*.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-268', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following **paranasal sinuses is the most commonly infected** one?',
  options:['Frontal','Maxillary','Ethmoid','Sphenoid'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The bank keys the ethmoid — and this is one to memorise as this paper prints it, because the answer usually taught elsewhere is the maxillary.**\n\n**⚠️ The ranking is a gap in the course, and it is filled here rather than declared.** No cached ENT deck ranks the sinuses by frequency of infection. `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` gives epidemiology only for the disease as a whole — *"nearly 1 in 7 (13.4%) of all adults aged ≥ 18 years were diagnosed with rhinosinusitis within the previous 12 months"*, *"adults between 45 and 74 years are most commonly affected"* — and never names a sinus; a grep of that deck for *maxillary* returns **zero**. `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` mentions the maxillary only inside a diagnostic criterion (*"Purulent mucus or edema in the middle meatus or anterior ethmoid"*), and `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` gives drainage sites, not frequencies. No other bank in the corpus asks this question either, so there is no printed answer to prefer over outside knowledge.\n\n**The case for the ethmoid** *(not taken from the course material)*: the ethmoid air cells are the **only sinuses reliably pneumatised at birth**, so in infants and small children they are the sinuses that can be infected at all — which is why acute ethmoiditis is the classic paediatric sinus infection and the classic cause of paediatric orbital cellulitis. They are also **anatomically unavoidable**: the anterior ethmoid cells sit inside the osteomeatal complex through which the frontal and maxillary sinuses must drain, so on CT they are involved in the great majority of episodes of rhinosinusitis at any age. On a *"which sinus shows disease most often"* reading, ethmoid is defensible and is what this paper is testing.\n\n**The case for the maxillary**, which is the more usual examination answer *(not taken from the course material)*: it is the **largest** sinus, its **ostium sits high on its medial wall** so it drains against gravity, and it alone can be infected from below by dental roots. It is the sinus most often symptomatic, most often tapped, and most often named in textbook lists of the commonest site of sinusitis.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Frontal** | absent at birth, pneumatises last (adolescence). Infected less often, but its complications are the feared ones — Pott’s puffy tumour and intracranial spread |\n| **Maxillary** | the answer most syllabuses print; largest sinus, high ostium, dental source. **Not keyed here** |\n| **Ethmoid** | **keyed** — present from birth, sits in the osteomeatal complex, the paediatric sinus infection |\n| **Sphenoid** | the least often infected in isolation, deepest and most hidden; its danger is its neighbours (optic nerve, cavernous sinus, pituitary) |\n\n**The discrepancy is recorded, not resolved, and the key is held as printed.** Answer *ethmoid* on this paper.\n\n**Bottom line: ethmoid is the sinus most often involved, maxillary the one most often blamed — know which answer the paper in front of you wants.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose1-45` and `entep-nose-45` (posterior ethmoid drainage and its pus), `entqb-nose1-4`, and `enthd-nose-264` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'This bank keys the ethmoid as the most commonly infected paranasal sinus — present from birth and lying inside the osteomeatal complex — against the maxillary that most syllabuses print.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.81 (Nose ch.1). **⚠️ Recorded discrepancy, key held as printed: the commonly taught answer is the maxillary sinus; this bank keys the ethmoid.** No cached ENT deck ranks the sinuses by frequency — verified by grepping `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` (zero hits for *maxillary*), `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` and `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` — and no corpus item in either other bank asks it. **Both cases are supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.** The rhinosinusitis prevalence figures quoted are from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-269', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'A **newborn infant** presents with **cyclic respiratory distress which is relieved by crying** but becomes **worse during breastfeeding**. What’s your provisional diagnosis?',
  options:['Unilateral choanal atresia','Bilateral choanal atresia','Pyriform aperture stenosis','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Cyclical distress relieved by crying and worsened by feeding is bilateral choanal atresia — the deck’s classic sign, with the feeding half added by the same physiology.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`: **"Bilateral choanal atresia: Symptomes of severe airway obstruction and cyclical cyanosis alleviated by crying are the classic signs of neonatal bilateral atresia."** The mechanism is on the physiology slide — **"Obligate nasal breathing for 6 to 8 WKs"** — so the only airway this baby has is the one crying forces open. Put a nipple in his mouth and even that closes: with both choanae blocked and the mouth occluded by feeding, there is no route for air at all, which is why feeding is the worst moment of the cycle *(the feeding link itself is not stated on the slide)*.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Unilateral choanal atresia** | the deck is explicit that it **"present[s] later in life with unilateral rhinorrhea"** — one patent side is enough to breathe through, so there is no neonatal distress and no cycle. It is also the commoner form: *"unilateral to bilateral cases is 2:1"*, and *"Unilat. atresia occurs more frequently on RT"* |\n| **Bilateral choanal atresia** | **keyed** — cyclical, relieved by crying, worse on feeding, present from birth |\n| **Pyriform aperture stenosis** | a genuine mimic, and the deck says so: **"Congenital nasal pyriform stenosis (CNPAS) — Rare cause of AW obstruction in infants, easily mistaken for choanal atresia."** It is excluded here on **rarity**, not on the story; the two are separated by CT — *"Height of nasal cavity is usually normal, width < 11mm in term infants is considered diagnostic"* |\n| **None of the above** | one option is the deck’s own named classic |\n\n**⚠️ Note what separates this item from its sibling.** `enthd-nose-265` gives the same cycle plus a **VSD murmur** and the same key; here the discriminator is **feeding**. Neither stem mentions noisy breathing, and that silence is itself diagnostic — the obstruction is above the larynx and behind the nose, so it produces no stridor.\n\n**Bottom line: bilateral atresia is a neonatal emergency, unilateral atresia is a childhood runny nose, and pyriform stenosis is the rare look-alike that CT settles.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose1-35` (the 2:1 ratio), `entqb-nose1-42`, `entqb-nose1-43`, `entqb-nose1-38`, `entep-nose-6` / `entep-nose-29` (the obsolete diagnostic test), and `enthd-nose-262` / `enthd-nose-265` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bilateral choanal atresia gives cyclical neonatal distress relieved by crying and worsened by feeding; the unilateral form presents years later with unilateral rhinorrhoea, and pyriform aperture stenosis is the rare CT-diagnosed mimic.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.82 (Nose ch.1). The classic-signs line, the unilateral-rhinorrhoea line, the 2:1 ratio, obligate nasal breathing and the CNPAS slide with its <11 mm criterion are all transcribed from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`. The reason feeding worsens the obstruction is added and is not on the slide.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-270', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following **nasal arteries arise from the internal carotid artery**?',
  options:['Sphenopalatine','Greater palatine','Ethmoidal arteries','Superior labial artery'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The ethmoidal arteries — they are the only nasal vessels that reach the nose from above, through the orbit, and the ophthalmic artery that gives them off is a branch of the internal carotid.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` prints the chain in full when it lists the four arteries of Kiesselbach’s plexus, and each line ends in the parent trunk:\n\n| Artery of the plexus | Chain, in the slide’s own words | Trunk |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Anterior ethmoidal** | *"branch of the ophthalmic artery → branch of the ICA"* | **internal carotid** |\n| **Sphenopalatine** | *"a terminal branch of the maxillary artery → a branch of the ECA"* | external carotid |\n| **Greater palatine** | *"a branch of the maxillary artery → a branch of the ECA"* | external carotid |\n| **Septal branch of superior labial** | *"a branch of the facial artery → branch of the ECA"* | external carotid |\n\nThe same deck gives the reason the nose bleeds so readily under *"Why bleeding from nose?"*: *"Highly vascular organ… Arterial to venous anastamoses… **ICA and ECA blood flow**"*. The nose is one of the few places in the body where the two carotid systems anastomose, and the ethmoidals are the internal carotid’s only contribution.\n\n**Why the other three cannot be:** all are external carotid vessels by the slide’s own chains — sphenopalatine and greater palatine through the **maxillary** artery, superior labial through the **facial** artery. `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` groups them the same way in its Little’s-area list: *"Anterior Ethmoid (Opth) · Superior Labial A (Facial) · Sphenopalatine A (IMAX) · Greater Palatine (IMAX)"*.\n\n**Why it matters surgically.** Because the ethmoidals come off the ophthalmic artery inside the orbit, they cannot be reached by ligating the external carotid — which is why `L25.1` lists **"Ethmoid ligation"** as a separate procedure from **"IMAX ligation"** and **"ECA ligation"**, and why an external carotid ligation does not stop a high septal or roof bleed.\n\n**Bottom line: everything into the nose is external carotid except the ethmoidals, which ride in from the orbit on the ophthalmic artery.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-53` and `entqb-nose1-21` (the **posterior** ethmoidal is the one **not** in Kiesselbach’s plexus), `entqb-nose1-44`, `entqb-nose2-70`, and `enthd-nose-278` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries reach the nose from the ophthalmic artery and so from the internal carotid; every other nasal artery is a branch of the external carotid through the maxillary or facial artery.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.82 (Nose ch.1). The four Kiesselbach chains with their parent trunks, *"ICA and ECA blood flow"* and the surgical ligation list are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; the parallel Little’s-area list is from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`. The point that an ECA ligation cannot control an ethmoidal bleed is added and is not stated on the slide.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-272', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following best describes the **function of the mucociliary nasal system**?',
  options:['Lubrication to avoid dryness','Shock absorption to reduce the effect of trauma','Cleaning of the mucosa and protection against foreign bodies and pathogens','Olfactory function'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The mucociliary system is a conveyor belt, not a lubricant — mucus traps what the inspired air carries and the cilia sweep it backwards to be swallowed.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` opens its rhinosinusitis section with a numbered list headed **"Defence- Mechanisms"**, and the mucociliary system is item one: *"**1. Mucociliary system**, 2. Epithelial integrity, 3. Reflexes (cough, sneeze), 4. Lysoszymes, opsonins & complement, 5. Immnoglobulins (secetory IgA, IgE), 6. Macrophages & lymphocytes."* Every item on that list is protective, which is the category the question is asking for.\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` says the same thing under function: **"Respiratory function: Air way, purification of air"** — purification, i.e. filtration and clearance, is what the mucus blanket and the cilia do together. The structure matches the job: the respiratory area is **"Lined by pseudo stratified columnar ciliated with goblet cells"** — goblet cells make the mucus, cilia move it.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Lubrication to avoid dryness** | mucus does keep the mucosa moist, and `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` calls the nose a *"Highly vascular organ secondary to incredible heating/humidification requirements"* — but humidification is the **blood supply and secretion** doing it, and the question asks specifically what the **mucociliary** apparatus is for. Cilia contribute nothing to lubrication |\n| **Shock absorption** | no slide anywhere describes a shock-absorbing role. Trauma protection in the nose is structural — the cartilaginous septum and the pyriform aperture — not mucosal |\n| **Cleaning and protection** | **keyed** — the deck’s first-listed defence mechanism, and *"purification of air"* |\n| **Olfactory function** | belongs to a **different part of the nose entirely**: *"The olfactory area: The upper part of the nose related to the superior turbinate, cribriform plate — lined by columnar **non ciliated** cells."* The olfactory epithelium has no cilia of this kind and no goblet cells |\n\n**Why it matters clinically.** Break the belt and the sinuses become infected: `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` lists **"Ciliary Impairment"** among the predisposing factors, naming **"Primary Ciliary"** dyskinesia; `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` treats in the same direction — *"Saline nasal irrigation: improves mucociliary clearance"*.\n\n**Bottom line: goblet cells make the flypaper, cilia move it, and anything that stops either causes sinusitis.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose4-148` (Grade Gain prints the swallowing interval) and `entqb-nose4-147` (ciliated sinus epithelium with goblet cells); the epithelium itself is `enthd-nose-279` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The mucociliary system is the nose’s first defence mechanism — mucus traps particles and pathogens and the cilia clear them — and its failure predisposes to rhinosinusitis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.82 (Nose ch.1). The six-item *"Defence- Mechanisms"* list is transcribed verbatim from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; *"purification of air"*, the respiratory and olfactory linings are from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; *"Ciliary Impairment"* and *"Primary Ciliary"* from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the saline-irrigation line from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; the humidification line from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-273', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following **group of lymph nodes drain the posterior aspect of the nasal cavity**?',
  options:['Submandibular lymph nodes','Supraclavicular lymph nodes','Upper deep cervical lymph nodes','Lower deep cervical lymph nodes'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The back of the nose drains with the nasopharynx — retropharyngeal nodes first, then the upper deep cervical chain.**\n\n**⚠️ Nasal lymphatic drainage is a gap in the course and is filled here rather than declared.** `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` covers walls, septum, blood supply, lining and function but prints **nothing** on lymphatics; a grep of all 34 cached ENT decks for *lymph* returns nasal drainage nowhere. The nearest printed anchor is `L1.2) Anatomy of the pharynx.pdf`, which gives the drainage of the neighbouring lymphoid tissue as **"Upper deep cervical LNs (esp. the Jugulo-diagastric LN)"** — the same destination, one structure over.\n\n**The rule, supplied** *(not taken from the course material)*: the nose drains in two directions along the line of its own development.\n\n| Part of the nose | First station | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Vestibule and external nose** | **submandibular** nodes (level Ib) | upper deep cervical |\n| **Anterior nasal cavity** | **submandibular** nodes | upper deep cervical |\n| **Posterior nasal cavity, choanae, nasopharynx** | **retropharyngeal** nodes | **upper deep cervical** (level II) |\n\nRetropharyngeal nodes are not offered here, so the correct answer among the four printed is the chain they empty into — the **upper deep cervical** group.\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Submandibular** | the **anterior** nose and vestibule, not the posterior cavity. This is the trap: it is the right answer to a different half of the same question |\n| **Supraclavicular** | the terminal node of thoracic and abdominal drainage (Virchow’s on the left). Involvement here from a head-and-neck primary means advanced, distant spread — never a first station |\n| **Upper deep cervical** | **keyed** — the outflow of the retropharyngeal nodes |\n| **Lower deep cervical** | levels III–IV, downstream of the upper group; reached late, and not the direct drainage of the nose |\n\n**Why it matters.** Posterior nasal and nasopharyngeal tumours metastasise **early and often bilaterally** to the upper deep cervical nodes precisely because the retropharyngeal route crosses the midline — which is why a posterior nasal mass with a neck node is staged as a nasopharyngeal disease, not a nasal one *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line: front of the nose to the submandibular nodes, back of the nose to retropharyngeal then upper deep cervical.** Cross-reference: the anterior nasal masses that present this way are covered in `entep-nose-2` and `entqb-nose2-60`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The posterior nasal cavity drains through the retropharyngeal nodes into the upper deep cervical chain, while the vestibule and anterior nasal cavity drain to the submandibular nodes.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.82 (Nose ch.1). **⚠️ Gap filled and tagged: no cached ENT deck prints the lymphatic drainage of the nose — verified by grepping all 34 caches for *lymph*, which returns the pharynx, larynx, ear and neck decks only.** The nearest printed anchor, *"Upper deep cervical LNs (esp. the Jugulo-diagastric LN)"* for the palatine tonsil, is from `L1.2) Anatomy of the pharynx.pdf`. The nasal drainage table itself is supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-274', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'**Ordinary sensation of the nose** is mainly derived from which of the following nerves?',
  options:['Olfactory nerve','Facial nerve','Trigeminal nerve','All of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Ordinary — that is, general — sensation is trigeminal. The olfactory nerve carries only smell, and it carries nothing else.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` draws the functional division of the nose explicitly and gives smell a **territory of its own**: *"**Olfactory function: in the olfactory area**"*, that area being *"The upper part of the nose related to the superior turbinate, cribriform plate — lined by columnar non ciliated cells"*. Everything else — *"Respiratory function: Air way, purification of air"*, *"Phonatory function"*, *"Reflex function"* — belongs to the rest of the cavity. The olfactory nerve is a **special sensory** nerve confined to that upper strip; it cannot mediate touch, pain or temperature anywhere.\n\n**⚠️ The nerve supply itself is not printed by any nose deck, and is supplied here** *(not taken from the course material)*. General sensation of the nose comes from **two divisions of the trigeminal**: the **ophthalmic (V1)** through its anterior ethmoidal branch, supplying the roof, the upper septum and the anterior lateral wall, and the **maxillary (V2)** through the nasopalatine and posterior nasal branches, supplying the rest. `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` confirms the nerve’s territory obliquely when it describes trigeminal neuralgia as affecting *"one or more divisions of the trigeminal nerve (usually maxillary or mandibular)"*.\n\n| Option | Its actual role in the nose |\n|---|---|\n| **Olfactory nerve** | **smell only**, and only from the olfactory area — no touch, no pain. Damage gives anosmia with normal nasal sensation |\n| **Facial nerve** | not sensory here at all: it is **secretomotor**, carrying parasympathetic fibres by the greater petrosal nerve to the pterygopalatine ganglion and so to the nasal glands *(not taken from the course material)*. It is the nerve of nasal **secretion**, not sensation |\n| **Trigeminal nerve** | **keyed** — V1 and V2 supply the whole nasal lining |\n| **All of the above** | would require the olfactory nerve to carry general sensation, which it cannot |\n\n**Why the distinction is clinically real.** Ammonia and menthol are perceived by a patient with complete anosmia, because those are **trigeminal irritants** rather than true odours — the classic bedside test for a feigned or non-organic loss of smell. Grade Gain prints exactly that idea in `entqb-nose2-80`, where a patient with anosmia *"could perceive"* something.\n\n**Bottom line: smell is CN I, feeling is CN V, watering is CN VII — three nerves, three jobs, one cavity.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-80`, `entqb-nose2-72`, and the sinus facial pain in `enthd-nose-266`’s neighbouring roof anatomy.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'General sensation of the nose is trigeminal (V1 anterior ethmoidal and V2 nasopalatine/posterior nasal); the olfactory nerve carries smell alone and the facial nerve supplies the nasal glands.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.83 (Nose ch.1). The olfactory area and the four nasal functions are transcribed from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; the trigeminal divisions line is from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`. **⚠️ Gap filled and tagged: no cached nose deck prints the sensory or secretomotor nerve supply of the nasal cavity, so V1/V2 territories, the greater petrosal pathway and the trigeminal-irritant test are supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-275', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following best describes the **function of the nasal turbinates (conchae)**?',
  options:['Increasing surface area of nasal mucosa for air conditioning','Creation of airway resistance to adjust air entry','Warming and humidifying the inspired air','All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All three describe the same organ doing the same job — the turbinates exist to put a large, warm, wet, resistive surface in the path of inspired air.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` builds the lateral wall out of them — *"The Lateral wall (3 turbinates & 3 meati)"* — and files the result under **"Respiratory function: Air way, purification of air."** `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` supplies the reason the nose is built this way, in its own words: the nose is a **"Highly vascular organ secondary to incredible heating/humidification requirements"**, with **"Vasculature runs just under mucosa"**. That is a description of a heat-and-moisture exchanger, and the turbinates are its radiator fins.\n\n| Option | Why it is true, not a distractor |\n|---|---|\n| **Increasing surface area for air conditioning** | three scrolls of bone folded into each cavity multiply the mucosal area many times over without lengthening the airway |\n| **Creation of airway resistance to adjust air entry** | the turbinates are **erectile**: their venous sinusoids engorge and shrink, which is what makes nasal obstruction *"Fluctuating"* and *"position-related"* in `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`, and what a decongestant reverses |\n| **Warming and humidifying** | the mechanism named by `L25.1`: dense submucosal vasculature immediately under the surface |\n| **All of the above** | **keyed** — no single option can be the answer, since each of the other three is true on its own |\n\n**The clinical corollary runs both ways, and the decks print both ends of it.** Turbinates that are too big obstruct: `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` lists **"Hypertrophy of the turbinates"** among the acquired structural causes of nasal obstruction. Turbinates that are destroyed obstruct too — paradoxically — because air conditioning fails: this is the empty-nose picture behind `entqb-nose9-263` (**over-resection of the inferior turbinate**) and the reason `entqb-nose9-262` asks for a **conservative** gold-standard treatment of turbinate hypertrophy rather than excision.\n\n**Bottom line: turbinates are surface area, resistance and a radiator all at once — which is why you reduce them and never remove them.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose9-262`, `entqb-nose9-263`, `entqb-nose3-110` (inferior turbinate hypertrophy in the elderly), `entqb-nose1-6` (the inferior meatus is the largest), and `enthd-nose-272` in this batch for the mucociliary half of the same job.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The turbinates increase mucosal surface area, generate the airway resistance that regulates air entry, and warm and humidify inspired air — one structure, three functions, which is why they are reduced rather than resected.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.83 (Nose ch.1). *"The Lateral wall (3 turbinates & 3 meati)"* and *"Respiratory function: Air way, purification of air"* are from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; the heating/humidification and submucosal vasculature lines from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; *"Hypertrophy of the turbinates"*, fluctuating and position-dependent obstruction from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. The erectile sinusoid mechanism and the empty-nose consequence are added and are not on the slides.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-276', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'All of the following are true concerning the **nasal vestibule except**?',
  options:['It’s lined with skin','It’s lined with respiratory epithelium','It’s the entrance to nasal cavity','It contains sebaceous glands and hair follicles'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The vestibule is lined with skin, not respiratory epithelium — it is outside the mucosa, and the change from one to the other happens at the limen nasi just inside it.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` places it and separates it from the lined cavity in three consecutive lines under *"The interior of the nose"*: **"The vestibule: The most anterior part of the nose"**, then **"The olfactory area… lined by columnar non ciliated cells"**, then **"The Respiratory area: The rest of the nasal cavity. Lined by pseudo stratified columnar ciliated with goblet cells."** The vestibule is deliberately given **no** epithelium in that list, because it is not part of either lined area — the deck defines the respiratory area as *the rest of the nasal cavity*, i.e. what is left after the vestibule and the olfactory strip.\n\nThe corpus supplies the lining directly: Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose1-20` asks *"The lining of the respiratory area is ________ while the vestibule is ________"* and keys **"Ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium; Stratified squamous epithelium"**. **Another bank’s printed answer, preferred here to outside reasoning.** `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` says the same thing about the region in passing when it explains why the nose bleeds: *"Vasculature runs just under mucosa (**not squamous**)"* — the squamous-lined part being precisely the vestibule.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Lined with skin** | **true** — stratified squamous, keratinised, continuous with the skin of the face over the alar rim |\n| **Lined with respiratory epithelium** | **false — keyed as the exception.** Respiratory epithelium starts **beyond** the limen nasi |\n| **The entrance to nasal cavity** | **true** — the deck’s own *"most anterior part of the nose"*, between the nostril and the limen |\n| **Contains sebaceous glands and hair follicles** | **true** — the vibrissae. It is skin, so it carries skin appendages *(the appendages are not named on any cached slide — supplied here)* |\n\n**Why this is more than a histology point.** Because the vestibule is skin, it gets **skin** diseases — furuncles, eczema, fissures — and it is the region where **finger-nail trauma** operates: `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` describes Little’s area as *"just above the vestibule"* and *"exposed to the drying effect of inspiratory current and to finger nail trauma"*. It also has no mucociliary clearance, so nothing swept back from the nose is cleared through it.\n\n**Bottom line: skin to the limen nasi, respiratory mucosa beyond it, olfactory mucosa at the roof.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose1-20`, and `enthd-nose-279` in this batch for the respiratory lining itself.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The nasal vestibule is skin-lined stratified squamous epithelium with hair follicles and sebaceous glands; respiratory epithelium begins beyond it at the limen nasi.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.83 (Nose ch.1). *"The vestibule: The most anterior part of the nose"*, the olfactory and respiratory linings are transcribed from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; *"Vasculature runs just under mucosa (not squamous)"* and the Little’s-area description from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **The vestibule’s squamous lining is not stated by any cached nose deck — taken instead from another bank’s printed key, `entqb-nose1-20`, in preference to outside knowledge.** The limen nasi and the skin appendages are added and tagged in the explanation.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-277', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'The **sphenoid sinus** is related to which of the following structures?',
  options:['Parotid gland','Lacrimal gland','Pituitary gland','Submandibular gland'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica directly on the roof of the sphenoid sinus — one thin plate of bone apart.**\n\n**⚠️ The relations of the sphenoid sinus are a gap in the cached nose decks and are supplied here** *(not taken from the course material)*. `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` mentions the sinus only for its drainage — **"Sphenoid sinus opens in the sphenoethmoidal recess"** — and a grep of all cached ENT decks for *pituitary*, *sella* and *hypophysis* returns **zero**. The nearest printed anchor is `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`, which lists the intracranial neighbours the sphenoid region can seed: *"Meningitis, intracranial abscess, and **cavernous sinus thrombosis** may develop along the course of the disease."*\n\n**The relations, supplied:** the sphenoid body is hollowed out into the sinus, and around it lie — **above**, the pituitary in its fossa and the optic chiasm; **laterally**, the **cavernous sinus** with the internal carotid artery, the optic nerve and cranial nerves III, IV, V1, V2 and VI grooving its walls; **below**, the roof of the nasopharynx. Nothing in the head has more dangerous neighbours per cubic centimetre.\n\n| Option | Where it actually sits |\n|---|---|\n| **Parotid gland** | over the ramus of the mandible in front of the ear — a lateral facial structure, nowhere near the skull base midline |\n| **Lacrimal gland** | supero-lateral **orbit**. Its duct system relates to the nose only at the far end, where the **nasolacrimal duct opens in the inferior meatus** (`L23`) |\n| **Pituitary gland** | **keyed** — in the sella turcica on the sinus roof |\n| **Submandibular gland** | in the submandibular triangle of the neck, below the mandible |\n\n**Two consequences follow directly from this relation, and both are examinable.** First, surgical: the sinus is the **corridor** for trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy — the pituitary is reached through the nose precisely because only the sinus roof lies between them. Second, pathological: sphenoid sinusitis is the sinus infection that threatens **vision and the cavernous sinus**, and its headache is felt deep — at the vertex or behind the eyes — rather than over a facial wall, which is why it is so often missed.\n\n**Bottom line: the sphenoid is a small sinus in a dangerous neighbourhood — pituitary above, cavernous sinus and optic nerve beside.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-72` (the site of sphenoid sinusitis headache), `entqb-nose1-45` and `entep-nose-45` (the posterior drainage sites), and `enthd-nose-264` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The pituitary gland lies in the sella turcica on the roof of the sphenoid sinus — the relation that makes trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy possible and sphenoid sinusitis dangerous.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.83 (Nose ch.1). **⚠️ Gap filled and tagged: no cached ENT deck prints the relations of the sphenoid sinus — grepping all 34 caches for *pituitar*, *sella* and *hypophys* returns zero, and no corpus item in either other bank asks it.** *"Sphenoid sinus opens in the sphenoethmoidal recess"* and the nasolacrimal duct line are from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; the intracranial complication list naming cavernous sinus thrombosis is from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`. The relations themselves, the trans-sphenoidal corridor and the headache site are supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-278', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'The **main blood supply of the nose originating from the external carotid artery** involves which of the following arteries?',
  options:['Maxillary and facial','Maxillary and superficial temporal','Superficial temporal and facial','Superficial temporal and superior thyroid'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Maxillary and facial — the two external carotid branches that actually enter the nose, and between them they supply three of the four arteries of Kiesselbach’s plexus.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` devotes a slide to each. Of the **facial artery**: *"The facial artery gives rise to the **superior labial artery**, the **lateral nasal artery**, and the **angular artery**. The superior labial artery gives off an alar branch and a septal branch… The lateral nasal artery supplies the alar cartilage on the external nose and also supplies the **nasal vestibule**. The angular artery supplies the external nasal tip, dorsum, and lateral wall."* Of the **maxillary artery** (IMAX), through its third part in the pterygopalatine fossa: *"IMAX terminal branches — Vidian (pterygoid canal), Palatovaginal (pharyngeal), **SPA**, Post. Lat nasal, Descending palatine."*\n\nThe plexus list makes the division explicit — three of four arteries are external carotid, and both trunks are represented:\n\n| Artery | Parent | Trunk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sphenopalatine | **maxillary** | ECA |\n| Greater palatine | **maxillary** | ECA |\n| Septal branch of superior labial | **facial** | ECA |\n| Anterior ethmoidal | ophthalmic | ICA |\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Maxillary and facial** | **keyed** |\n| **Maxillary and superficial temporal** | the **superficial temporal** is the ECA’s *other* terminal branch (`entqb-nose1-44`: *"terminal branches of the external carotid artery are superficial temporal & maxillary"*), but it runs up in front of the ear to the **scalp**. It contributes nothing to the nose |\n| **Superficial temporal and facial** | drops the maxillary — the single most important nasal trunk, and the one carrying the sphenopalatine, *"the artery of epistaxis"* (`entep-nose-3`, `entep-mfe5-16`) |\n| **Superficial temporal and superior thyroid** | the superior thyroid is the ECA’s **first** branch and supplies the thyroid and larynx; neither vessel reaches the nose |\n\n**⚠️ Read the stem carefully.** It asks only about the **external carotid** contribution. The nose’s full supply also includes the **ethmoidal arteries** from the ophthalmic and so from the ICA — that is `enthd-nose-270`, the mirror item on the same page — and `L25.1` prints the dual origin as a heading of its own: *"**ICA and ECA blood flow**"*.\n\n**Bottom line: maxillary from behind and below, facial from in front — plus ethmoidals from above, which the ECA does not own.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose1-44`, `entep-nose-53`, `entqb-nose1-21`, `entqb-nose2-49`, `entqb-nose2-67`, and `enthd-nose-270` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The external carotid supplies the nose through the maxillary artery (sphenopalatine, greater palatine, posterior lateral nasal) and the facial artery (superior labial, lateral nasal, angular); the superficial temporal supplies the scalp, not the nose.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.83 (Nose ch.1). The facial-artery slide, the IMAX terminal-branch list, the four Kiesselbach chains and *"ICA and ECA blood flow"* are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; the ECA terminal branches are printed by Grade Gain in `entqb-nose1-44`. **Deliberate mirror of `enthd-nose-270` on the same page, which asks the internal carotid half.** The superficial temporal and superior thyroid territories are added and are not on the nose slides.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-279', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **type of epithelium lining the respiratory area of the nose**?',
  options:['Non ciliated columnar epithelium','Ciliated columnar epithelium','Non keratinized stratified squamous epithelium','Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Ciliated columnar — the deck’s full term is pseudostratified columnar ciliated with goblet cells, and the cilia are the word that decides this question.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` divides the interior of the nose into three, and gives each its own lining:\n\n| Region | Slide’s own words | Epithelium |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Vestibule** | *"The most anterior part of the nose"* | skin — stratified squamous *(lining not printed on this slide; see `enthd-nose-276`)* |\n| **Olfactory area** | *"The upper part of the nose related to the superior turbinate, cribriform plate"* | *"lined by **columnar non ciliated** cells"* |\n| **Respiratory area** | *"The rest of the nasal cavity"* | *"Lined by **pseudo stratified columnar ciliated with goblet cells**"* |\n\n**⚠️ This is one half of a deliberate contrast pair, and the two halves share one menu.** The book prints the identical four options for the **olfactory** area as Q238 (`enthd-nose-238`, batch 1), where the key is **non ciliated** columnar. Change one word in the stem — *olfactory* for *respiratory* — and the key moves from option A to option B. Nothing else differs.\n\n**Why the difference exists.** The respiratory area has a job that needs movement: mucus from the goblet cells traps particles and the cilia sweep the blanket backwards to the pharynx to be swallowed — the mucociliary system of `enthd-nose-272`. The olfactory area has the opposite requirement: odorant molecules must reach the receptors and stay there long enough to be sensed, so a sweeping ciliated carpet would be counter-productive; its columnar cells are non-ciliated in the deck’s sense, carrying olfactory receptor processes instead.\n\n| Option | Where it is actually found |\n|---|---|\n| **Non ciliated columnar** | the **olfactory area** — Q238’s key, not this one |\n| **Ciliated columnar** | **keyed** — the respiratory area, and the sinuses too (`entqb-nose4-147`: *ciliated epithelium containing goblet cells*) |\n| **Non keratinized stratified squamous** | oropharynx, oesophagus, true vocal cord (`entqb-thr1-23`) — a wear-resistant wet lining, not a clearing one |\n| **Keratinized stratified squamous** | skin, and therefore the **nasal vestibule** — the third region, excluded by the stem |\n\n**Bottom line: three regions, three linings — skin in front, cilia through the middle, non-ciliated columnar at the roof.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose1-20` (respiratory versus vestibule in one item), `entqb-nose4-147`, `entqb-nose4-148`, `enthd-nose-276`, `enthd-nose-272`, and the paired `enthd-nose-238`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The respiratory area of the nose is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells, against the non-ciliated columnar lining of the olfactory area and the squamous skin of the vestibule.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.84 (Nose ch.1). The three-region interior slide with both linings is transcribed verbatim from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with Q238 (`enthd-nose-238`, batch 1), which prints the identical four options for the olfactory area and keys the non-ciliated columnar line — recorded in house-sweep-nose.md; the two must not be merged.** The functional reason for the difference is added and is not on the slide.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-280', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'An **11-year-old child** presents to the ENT clinic with **mild nasal bleeding**. What is the **most common site of origin** for the bleeding in this child?',
  options:['Nasal roof','Nasal floor','Little’s area','Nasal vestibule'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Little’s area — the deck names it as the usual bleeding site in children in exactly these words.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` prints the anatomy and the epidemiology on the same slide: **"It is situated in the anterior inferior part of nasal septum, just above the vestibule. Four arteries anastomose to form a vascular plexus called ‘Kiesselbach’s plexus.’ This area is exposed to the drying effect of inspiratory current and to finger nail trauma, and is **the usual site for epistaxis in children and young adults**."** The age curve is on the opening slide: *"Epistaxis may occur at any age with a bimodal distribution with two peaks: The first peak is during **childhood usually from the Little’s area**. The second peak is in the fifth or sixth decades and is frequently associated with hypertension."*\n\n**Three features of the site explain both the frequency and the mildness.** It is **anterior**, so it is dried by every inspired breath; it is **reachable**, so a fingernail reaches it; and it is an **anastomosis of four arteries**, so it is unusually vascular for its size. Anterior bleeding is correspondingly *"typically less severe"* and *"most common (>90%)"* — which matches the **mild** bleed in this stem.\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Nasal roof** | the ethmoidal territory. Bleeding here is uncommon, is fed from the ophthalmic artery, and is **not** controllable by anterior pressure or an ECA ligation — a serious bleed, not a child’s nosebleed |\n| **Nasal floor** | poorly vascular and not a named bleeding area on any slide. The deck’s four *"Bleeding areas"* are **"Kesselbach’s plexus, Woodruffs plexus, Sphenopalatine area, Ethmoidal vessels"** — the floor is not among them |\n| **Little’s area** | **keyed** |\n| **Nasal vestibule** | the closest wrong answer, and the trap: Little’s area lies *"just above the vestibule"*, not in it. The vestibule is **skin**, and `L25.1` makes exactly this point — *"Vasculature runs just under mucosa (not squamous)"* |\n\n**Bottom line: a child with a mild nosebleed is bleeding from Little’s area until shown otherwise — anterior, septal, and stopped by pinching the soft part of the nose.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-68` (most common site of anterior epistaxis), `entqb-nose2-52`, `entep-nose-89`, `entep-nose-53` and `entqb-nose1-21` (the four arteries), and `enthd-nose-284` / `enthd-nose-286` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Little’s area on the anterior-inferior septum, just above the vestibule, is the usual site of epistaxis in children and young adults — anterior, dried by inspired air, reachable by a fingernail and fed by four anastomosing arteries.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.84 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). The Little’s-area slide, the bimodal age distribution, the four named bleeding areas, the >90% anterior figure and *"not squamous"* are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-281', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'A **15-year-old boy** presents with **epistaxis that has stopped a few hours ago**. He has bled the equivalent of **1.5 glasses** and has had **similar attacks over the past 3 months** for which he was managed conservatively. **Bleeding always starts from the left side and soon becomes bilateral.** Clinical examination reveals blood in the nasal cavities and his **vital signs are within normal**. What is the **best next step**?',
  options:['Emergency sphenopalantine coagulation','Suction of blood and cauterization of Little’s area','Anterior and posterior nasal packing','CT nose and paranasal sinuses with contrast','MRI nose and paranasal sinuses with contrast'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Recurrent severe epistaxis in an adolescent male is juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma until imaging says otherwise — and the bleeding has already stopped, so the next step is the scan, not haemostasis.**\n\n**Two facts in the stem decide it.** First, the demographic: `L26) nasal masses.pdf` describes angiofibroma as a *"Lobulated mass that arises in the confluence of posterolateral nasal wall and lateral nasopharynx (centered on SPF)"* occurring in **"Males during adolescence"**, presenting with **"Nasal Obstruction (Unilateral/Bilateral), Epistaxis, Ear Fullness, Facial Asymmetry"**. Second, the pattern: bleeding that **starts on one side and becomes bilateral**, recurrent over months, is a **unilateral posterior** source overflowing across the choanae. `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` lists exactly these as its **"Prognostic factors (omens)"** — *"I: Unilaterality, II: Frequency, III: Amount, IV: Duration, V: Accompanying complaints"* — and adds **"Middle age—tumors"** to its age table, of which this is the adolescent counterpart.\n\n**Why imaging, and why CT.** The patient is **not bleeding now** and is **haemodynamically normal**, so no emergency haemostatic manoeuvre is indicated; what is indicated is finding the cause of three months of recurrence. `L26` prints the work-up in order — *"Diagnostic Work-up: **Biopsy ??**, Computed Tomography, CT angiography, Magnetic resonance imaging"*. The **"Biopsy ??"** is the deck’s own warning: this lesion is **"Mainly supplied from ECA (IMAX)"** and biopsy risks catastrophic haemorrhage; the diagnosis is made by **imaging**. CT comes first because the diagnostic signs are bony — *"Bowing of post maxillary wall (**Holman Miller sign**)"* and *"Widening of SPF"*.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the best next step |\n|---|---|\n| **Emergency sphenopalatine coagulation** | there is no emergency: bleeding stopped hours ago and vitals are normal. `L25.1` reserves surgical control for *"Continued bleeding despite nasal packing"* and *"Pt requires transfusion"*. Operating blind on an unimaged vascular tumour is also the worst possible order of events. *(The option is printed "sphenopalantine"; the correct spelling is sphenopalatine.)* |\n| **Suction and cauterization of Little’s area** | treats the wrong site. Little’s area is **anterior**; this is a posterior bleed that becomes bilateral, and cauterising an area that is not bleeding achieves nothing while missing the tumour |\n| **Anterior and posterior nasal packing** | a treatment for **active** posterior bleeding, with the complication list `L25.1` prints — *"Uncomfortable, Hospitalization, ET dysfunction and hemotympanum, Hypoxia and cerebral ischemia, Myocardial infarction, Toxic shock syndrome, Failure rate = 20–50%"*. Inflicting that on a boy who is not bleeding is unjustifiable |\n| **CT nose and paranasal sinuses with contrast** | **keyed** — shows the mass, its bony signs and its vascularity, and maps it before anyone touches it |\n| **MRI with contrast** | genuinely useful, and on the deck’s own list — *"T1 = intermediate signal, T2 = heterogenous enhancement with signal void (salt and pepper appearance), T1 gad = prominent enhancement"* — but it comes **after** CT, for intracranial and soft-tissue extension. It is the second-best answer, not the wrong one |\n\n**Bottom line: adolescent male + recurrent unilateral epistaxis = angiofibroma; image it, and do not biopsy it.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-59`, `entep-nose-82`, `entep-nose-57` and `entep-throat-127` (the tumour’s features), `entep-nose-113` (proptosis from extension), `entep-throat-119`, `entep-enr-22` and `entep-enr-25` (the same "next step" question), and `entep-nose-41` / `entep-nose-42`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A haemodynamically stable adolescent male with months of recurrent unilateral-then-bilateral epistaxis needs contrast CT for juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma — not cautery, not packing, and never a biopsy first.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.84 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). **Printed typo kept as transcribed: option A reads "sphenopalantine"; the correct spelling is sphenopalatine — noted once in the explanation.** The angiofibroma description, presentation list, work-up order with *"Biopsy ??"*, Holman Miller sign, MRI signal characteristics and ECA/IMAX supply are transcribed from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; the prognostic omens, the age table, the surgical indications and the posterior-pack complication list from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **Straddles `ent-nasalmass`; filed to `ent-epistaxis` because the question is asked and answered as an epistaxis work-up.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-282', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'**True or False:** Blood **originating from the nose** but **coming out of the mouth or swallowed and passed in the stool** is considered **epistaxis**.',
  options:['True','False'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**True — epistaxis is defined by where the blood comes FROM, not by where it comes out.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` gives the definition in one line and it contains no exit route at all: **"Epistaxis = bleeding from the nose."** Origin is the whole of it.\n\n**The anatomy makes the alternative routes ordinary rather than exceptional.** The nasal cavity opens backwards into the nasopharynx through the choanae, so blood from a posterior source runs down the pharynx instead of out of the nostril. `L25.1` defines posterior epistaxis in exactly those terms — *"bleeding that arises from so far posterior that the bleeding site can not be identified by anterior inspection or controlled by anterior pack"* — and it is why the deck’s first-aid instructions tell the patient to **"Spit out any blood"** and to sit *"leaning forward, breathing through the mouth"*: because swallowing it is the default.\n\n**Why the point is worth a whole question.** Swallowed blood is invisible blood. A posterior bleed can be losing volume steadily with almost nothing on the handkerchief, then declare itself as **haematemesis** (blood is a gastric irritant and is vomited) or as **melaena** days later. Treating those as gastrointestinal bleeds sends the patient to the wrong specialty; recognising them as epistaxis is what puts the endoscope in the nose. The same reasoning is why `L25.1` puts *"Vital signs—need IV?"* and a cross-match on its initial-management list rather than relying on the visible loss.\n\n**Bottom line: follow the blood upstream, not downstream — the source names the diagnosis.** Paired directly with `enthd-nose-283`, which asks the converse and keys **False**; together they define epistaxis by origin from both directions.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Epistaxis is defined by nasal origin, not by the route the blood takes out — so blood from the nose that is swallowed, vomited or passed as melaena is still epistaxis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, pp.84–85 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis) — printed as a True/False item with two options. *"Epistaxis = bleeding from the nose"*, the definition of posterior epistaxis, *"Spit out any blood"*, the sitting-forward instruction and the initial-management list are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **Deliberate converse pair with `enthd-nose-283`.** Haematemesis and melaena as presentations of swallowed nasal blood are added and are not on the slide.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-283', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'**True or False:** Blood **originating from the stomach or oesophagus** but **coming out of the nose** is considered **epistaxis**.',
  options:['True','False'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**False — this is the converse of the previous item, and the same definition settles it: origin, not exit.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`: **"Epistaxis = bleeding from the nose."** Blood welling up from the oesophagus or stomach is **haematemesis** that has regurgitated through the nasopharynx into the nose; the nose is only a spout. Nothing in the nasal mucosa is bleeding, so nothing in the nose needs treating — and packing it would be both useless and dangerous.\n\n**How the two are told apart at the bedside** *(not taken from the course material — the deck defines epistaxis but does not print a differential against haematemesis)*: nasal blood is bright red, arrives with the patient upright, and is seen on anterior rhinoscopy or endoscopy coming from a nasal site; gastric blood is darker, often coffee-ground, arrives with retching, and leaves the nasal cavity **clean** on inspection. The deck’s own examination sequence is what makes the call — *"Physical exam: Anterior rhinoscopy, Endoscopy"* — because a nose with no bleeding point and no blood on its walls is not the source.\n\n**Why the distinction is not pedantic.** Mistaking upper-gastrointestinal bleeding for epistaxis delays resuscitation and endoscopy in a patient who may be bleeding from varices or an ulcer, while the clinician packs a healthy nose. The error is easy to make, because a large upper-GI bleed genuinely does come out of the nose in a supine or vomiting patient.\n\n**Bottom line: blood in the nose is not the same as blood from the nose.** Paired directly with `enthd-nose-282`, which asks the mirror question and keys **True** — the two together define epistaxis strictly by origin.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Blood from the stomach or oesophagus that exits through the nose is haematemesis, not epistaxis — epistaxis is defined by nasal origin alone.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.85 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis) — printed as a True/False item with two options; the printed stem spells it "esophagus". *"Epistaxis = bleeding from the nose"* and *"Physical exam: Anterior rhinoscopy, Endoscopy"* are from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **Deliberate converse pair with `enthd-nose-282`.** The bedside differentiation from haematemesis is supplied from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-284', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'All of the following are true regarding **anterior epistaxis except**?',
  options:['It occurs mostly in children','It’s mild and can be managed in the office','It’s the most prevalent form of epistaxis','It originates mainly from sphenopalatine artery'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The sphenopalatine artery is the artery of POSTERIOR epistaxis — anterior bleeding comes from Kiesselbach’s plexus on the septum.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` prints the two-column comparison the question is built from, and every true option is a phrase off it:\n\n| | Anterior | Posterior |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Slide’s words** | *"younger, usually septal from Kiesselbachs plexus, most common (>90%), typically less severe"* | *"This comes most commonly from the **SPA** or one of its branches. It is a common site of epistaxis in elderly hypertensive patients"* |\n| **Age** | children and young adults — *"The first peak is during childhood usually from the Little’s area"* | fifth or sixth decade, *"frequently associated with hypertension"* |\n| **Severity** | mild; office cautery or an anterior pack | severe; *"can not be… controlled by anterior pack"*, needs admission |\n\nSo options A, B and C are all printed teaching. Option D takes the **posterior** line and attaches it to the anterior column — the single substitution the item is testing.\n\n**⚠️ One nuance, because the option is not absurd.** The sphenopalatine artery **is** one of the four arteries of Kiesselbach’s plexus — `L25.1` lists *"Sphenopalatine artery → a terminal branch of the maxillary artery → a branch of the ECA"* among them — so it does contribute to Little’s area through its septal branch. What is false is **"mainly"**: the plexus is an anastomosis of four vessels and no single one dominates it, whereas posterior bleeding really is *"most commonly from the SPA"*. The word carrying the error is the adverb, not the artery.\n\n**Office management, for completeness.** `L25.1` sets it out as *"Clear the clots → Cotton soaked with decongestant → Cautery → Ant. Nasal pack"*, cautery being **"Silver nitrate sticks… applied over the Little’s Area"** until the mucosa *"becomes discoloured to a grey-white"*.\n\n**Bottom line: anterior = septal = Kiesselbach = mild = children; posterior = SPA = severe = elderly hypertensive.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-52`, `entqb-nose2-68`, `entqb-nose2-71`, `entep-nose-3` and `entqb-nose2-49` (the SPA as *the artery of epistaxis*), and `enthd-nose-285` in this batch, which asks the posterior half.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Anterior epistaxis is septal, from Kiesselbach’s plexus, mild, commonest (>90%) and typical of children; it is posterior epistaxis that arises mainly from the sphenopalatine artery.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.85 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). The anterior-versus-posterior comparison slides, the bimodal age peaks, the Kiesselbach artery chains and the office-management sequence with silver nitrate are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **The caveat that the sphenopalatine artery does contribute to Kiesselbach’s plexus is recorded; the key is sound because the stem says "mainly".** Deliberate pair with `enthd-nose-285`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-285', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'All of the following are true regarding **posterior epistaxis except**?',
  options:['It occurs mostly in hypertensive old aged patients','It’s severe and needs hospital administration for management','It’s less common that anterior variety','It’s almost always preceded by a trauma'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Posterior epistaxis is characteristically SPONTANEOUS — in an elderly hypertensive patient it arrives without any trauma at all, and trauma is a feature of the anterior, paediatric variety.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` supplies each true option directly. On the anterior-versus-posterior slide: *"Posterior: This comes most commonly from the SPA or one of its branches. It is a **common site of epistaxis in elderly hypertensive patients**"*, against the anterior form as *"most common (>90%)"* — so posterior is by arithmetic the **less** common. On the opening slide: *"The second peak is in the fifth or sixth decades and is **frequently associated with hypertension**"*, and on the etiology slide, in four words, *"**Severe + elderly patient = hypertension**"*. On admission: the posterior-pack complication list begins *"Uncomfortable, **Hospitalization**"*, and the prerequisites slide adds *"Elderly and those with other chronic diseases may need to be **admitted to the ICU**, Continuous cardiopulmonary monitoring, Antibiotics, Oxygen supplementation… IVF"*.\n\n**Where trauma actually sits in the deck.** Under *"Etiology and Age"*: **"Children—foreign body, nose picking"**, **"Adults—trauma, idiopathic"**, *"Middle age—tumors"*, *"Old age--hypertension"*. Trauma is an **adult** and **anterior** mechanism — nail trauma to Little’s area, which the deck describes as *"exposed to the drying effect of inspiratory current and to finger nail trauma"*. A finger cannot reach the posterior nasal cavity, which is the simplest way to see why the option is false.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Hypertensive, old aged** | **true** — the deck’s second peak, stated twice |\n| **Severe, needs hospital admission** | **true** — hospitalization and possible ICU are printed prerequisites of a posterior pack |\n| **Less common than anterior** | **true** — anterior is *">90%"*, so posterior is under 10% |\n| **Almost always preceded by trauma** | **false — keyed.** Posterior bleeding is typically spontaneous; trauma belongs to the anterior septum |\n\n**Two printed slips in this item, kept as transcribed:** option B reads *"hospital administration"* for **admission**, and option C reads *"less common that anterior"* for **than**. Neither changes the meaning or the key.\n\n**Bottom line: posterior bleeds are spontaneous, severe, elderly, hypertensive and uncommon — and they are the ones that get admitted.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-57` and `entep-nose-31` (hypertension in the elderly), `entqb-nose2-71`, `entqb-nose2-67` (which artery to ligate), `entep-nose-14` and `entep-nose-68` (posterior bleeds that resist an anterior pack), and `enthd-nose-284` / `enthd-nose-288` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Posterior epistaxis is spontaneous rather than traumatic — severe, uncommon (<10%), from the sphenopalatine artery, in elderly hypertensive patients, and it needs admission.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.85 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). **Printed slips kept as transcribed: "hospital administration" for admission, and "less common that anterior" for than — noted once in the explanation.** The posterior definition, the elderly-hypertensive line, *"Severe + elderly patient = hypertension"*, the >90% anterior figure, the *"Etiology and Age"* table and the posterior-pack complications and prerequisites are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. Deliberate pair with `enthd-nose-284`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-286', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common cause of epistaxis in children**?',
  options:['Idiopathic','Nasal picking / foreign body insertion','Trauma','Hypertension'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Idiopathic — most childhood nosebleeds have no demonstrable cause at all, and the bank ranks nasal picking second, not first.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` opens its etiology section with a slide whose entire content is two lines: **"Idiopathic"** and *"Severe + elderly patient = hypertension"*. Idiopathic then heads the formal four-way classification — *"I: **Idiopathic**, II: Local factors, III: Systemic factors, IV: Environmental"* — with trauma, foreign bodies and nose picking all sitting **inside** category II: *"Local factors — Trauma, Infectious/Inflammatory, Neoplasm, Vascular, Iatrogenic, Structural, **Foreign Bodies**, Nasal sprays, other."*\n\n**Another bank prints the same key, which settles it.** The endpoint file’s `entep-nose-61` asks *"The most common cause of epistaxis in children is"* over Atrophic rhinitis / Idiopathic / Bleeding tendency / Vascular tumours and keys **Idiopathic**. Grade Gain keys idiopathic for the unqualified question too, in `entqb-nose2-56` and `entqb-nose1-23`.\n\n**⚠️ This is one half of a deliberate pair and the halves must not be merged.** The book prints `enthd-nose-287` immediately after it, asking the **2nd** most common cause of epistaxis in children over a nearly identical menu, and keying **nasal picking / foreign body insertion**. The two questions are a single ranking split across two items:\n\n| Rank in children | Cause | Item |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1st** | **Idiopathic** — no bleeding point, no systemic cause found | **this question** |\n| **2nd** | **Nasal picking / foreign body insertion** | `enthd-nose-287` |\n\nAnswering *nasal picking* here is the intended error, and it is intended precisely because that is the correct answer to the very next question.\n\n**⚠️ A tension inside the deck, recorded and not resolved.** The same lecture’s *"Etiology and Age"* table reads **"Children—foreign body, nose picking (1/3 with chronic bleeds have coagulation d/o)"** and reserves *"idiopathic"* for adults. Read alone, that slide would key option B. The bank follows the etiology slide’s ranking and the endpoint file agrees with it, so the key stands — but the age table is what makes this question genuinely hard.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Idiopathic** | **keyed** — the deck’s first-listed cause and the endpoint file’s printed key |\n| **Nasal picking / foreign body** | the **second** commonest; the key of the paired item |\n| **Trauma** | a local factor, and the deck’s adult mechanism |\n| **Hypertension** | the **old-age** cause: *"Old age--hypertension"*. Essentially never the cause in a child |\n\n**Bottom line: in a child, most nosebleeds are idiopathic and the commonest identifiable cause is a fingernail.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-61`, `entqb-nose2-56`, `entqb-nose1-23`, `entqb-nose2-58` (which asks for *a* common cause and keys nose picking), and `enthd-nose-287` / `enthd-nose-288` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The commonest cause of epistaxis in children is idiopathic; nasal picking and foreign bodies come second — and this bank tests the ranking as two consecutive questions.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.85 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). The *"Idiopathic"* etiology slide, the four-way classification, the local-factors list, the *"Etiology and Age"* table and *"Old age--hypertension"* are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **The endpoint file prints the same key in `entep-nose-61` — another bank’s printed answer, preferred to outside reasoning.** **⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with `enthd-nose-287` (2nd most common); recorded in house-sweep-nose.md, must not be merged.** **⚠️ Internal deck tension recorded, key not disputed: the *Etiology and Age* slide names foreign body and nose picking for children and idiopathic for adults.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-287', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **2nd most common cause of epistaxis in children**?',
  options:['Idiopathic','Nasal picking / foreign body insertion','Tumors','Hypertension'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Nasal picking and foreign body insertion — second only to "no cause found", and the commonest identifiable cause in a child.**\n\nThis is the lower half of the ranking set out in `enthd-nose-286`, the item printed immediately above it; the ranking table and the deck tension are there and are not repeated. The line that decides this question is `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`’s age table: **"Children—foreign body, nose picking (1/3 with chronic bleeds have coagulation d/o)"**, with *"Foreign Bodies"* also listed among the **local factors** of the four-way etiology.\n\n**The anatomy explains why a fingernail is so effective.** Little’s area lies *"in the anterior inferior part of nasal septum, just above the vestibule"* and is *"exposed to the drying effect of inspiratory current and to **finger nail trauma**"* — an anastomosis of four arteries sitting immediately under thin mucosa, within reach of a child’s finger. `L25.1` calls it *"the usual site for epistaxis in children and young adults"*.\n\n**⚠️ Note the menu change between the pair.** Q286 offered *Trauma* as its third option; this item swaps it for **Tumors**. Only the option set moves — the ranking is the same one, split across two questions, and the two must not be merged.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Idiopathic** | the **1st** most common — the key of `enthd-nose-286`. Choosing it here is the intended error |\n| **Nasal picking / foreign body** | **keyed** — the commonest identifiable cause in childhood |\n| **Tumors** | the deck assigns these to a different decade: *"**Middle age—tumors**"*. The childhood exception is juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma in adolescent boys (`enthd-nose-281`), which is a cause of **severe recurrent** epistaxis, not of the ordinary childhood nosebleed |\n| **Hypertension** | *"Old age--hypertension"*; not a paediatric cause |\n\n**A parenthesis on the slide that is easy to miss and worth carrying.** *"1/3 with chronic bleeds have coagulation d/o"* — a third of children with **recurrent** epistaxis have an underlying coagulopathy, which is why `L25.1` orders *"Lab Tests (For severe or recurrent epistaxis): CBC, Coagulation profile, Liver functions tests, Creatinine, Cross Match."* A single nosebleed needs no blood tests; a pattern of them does.\n\n**Bottom line: idiopathic first, fingernail second — and if the fingernail story keeps repeating, check the clotting.** Cross-references: `enthd-nose-286`, `entqb-nose2-58`, `entqb-nose2-69` (how a child’s primary epistaxis is treated), `entep-nose-18` (unilateral foul discharge from a nasal foreign body), and `enthd-nose-280` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Nasal picking and foreign body insertion are the second commonest cause of childhood epistaxis after idiopathic — and a third of children with recurrent bleeds have a coagulation disorder.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.85 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). The *"Etiology and Age"* table with its coagulation parenthesis, the local-factors list, the Little’s-area slide and the lab-test list are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with `enthd-nose-286` (most common); recorded in house-sweep-nose.md, must not be merged — the option sets differ only in Trauma versus Tumors.** **The shared ranking table lives in `enthd-nose-286`; this entry points at it rather than repeating it.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-288', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common cause of epistaxis in old-aged adults**?',
  options:['Trauma','Nasal picking','Hypertension','Angiofibroma'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Hypertension — the deck says it three separate times, and it is the second peak of the bimodal age curve.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` states it on the opening slide — *"Epistaxis may occur at any age with a bimodal distribution with two peaks… The **second peak is in the fifth or sixth decades and is frequently associated with hypertension**"* — again on the etiology slide, whose whole content is *"Idiopathic"* and **"Severe + elderly patient = hypertension"** — and a third time in the *"Etiology and Age"* table: **"Old age--hypertension"**. It also underlies the anterior/posterior split: *"Posterior… is a common site of epistaxis in **elderly hypertensive patients**"*, and the systemic-factors list pairs *"**Atherosclerosis & hypertension**"* as one entry, since the stiff, atheromatous vessel is what fails to retract when it tears.\n\n| Option | Which decade it belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Trauma** | *"**Adults**—trauma, idiopathic"* — the deck’s adult mechanism, not the elderly one |\n| **Nasal picking** | *"**Children**—foreign body, nose picking"*; the key of `enthd-nose-287` |\n| **Hypertension** | **keyed** — *"Old age--hypertension"* |\n| **Angiofibroma** | the adolescent-male tumour of `L26) nasal masses.pdf` — *"Males during adolescence"*. Wrong sex-and-age band entirely, and in the elderly a nasal tumour causing epistaxis would be a **carcinoma**, not an angiofibroma |\n\n**Why the elderly bleed differently as well as more.** The elderly bleed is characteristically **posterior**, from the sphenopalatine artery, so it cannot be seen on anterior rhinoscopy or stopped by an anterior pack; it is heavier; and the patient is least able to tolerate the loss. That is why `L25.1` reserves its most cautious protocol for exactly this group — *"Elderly and those with other chronic diseases may need to be admitted to the ICU, Continuous cardiopulmonary monitoring, Antibiotics, Oxygen supplementation may be needed, Mild sedation/analgesia, IVF"* — and why the posterior pack’s own complication list includes *"Hypoxia and cerebral ischemia"* and *"Myocardial infarction"*.\n\n**⚠️ A note on the physiology, since the deck asserts the association without explaining it** *(not taken from the course material)*: hypertension is better understood as a factor that **prolongs and worsens** a bleed than as the event that starts it, and many elderly patients are additionally on aspirin or an anticoagulant — both on the deck’s own drug list, *"Drugs (aspirin, NSAID, anticoagulants, nasal steroids)"*. The association is real and is what the exam wants; the causal arrow is softer than the phrasing suggests.\n\n**Bottom line: childhood peak from Little’s area, old-age peak from the sphenopalatine artery in a hypertensive — two peaks, two sites, two levels of danger.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-57`, `entep-nose-31`, `entep-mfe4-13`, `entep-nose-14`, `entep-nose-68`, and `enthd-nose-285` / `enthd-nose-286` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Hypertension is the commonest cause of epistaxis in old age — the second peak of the bimodal curve, characteristically posterior, from the sphenopalatine artery, and the one that needs admission.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.85 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). The bimodal distribution, *"Severe + elderly patient = hypertension"*, *"Old age--hypertension"*, the systemic-factors and drug lists, the posterior-pack prerequisites and complications are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; *"Males during adolescence"* for angiofibroma from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`. The caveat on the causal direction of hypertension is added and tagged in the explanation.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-289', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'All of the following are among the **first aid procedures done in case of epistaxis except**?',
  options:['Head looking forwards','Prone position','Nose pinch','Decongestant nasal drops'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Lying the patient prone is wrong — epistaxis first aid puts the patient SITTING UP and leaning forward, and the deck says so in a sentence.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` prints the initial-management list: *"ABC’s, Medical history/Medications, Vital signs—need IV?, **Head bent forward**, Cold compresses, **Spit out any blood**, **Nose pinching**"*, and then the instruction in full: **"Direct digital pressure to the lower soft cartilaginous part of the nose by pinching for approximately 10 minutes is the method of choice. The patient should be **sitting and leaning forward**, breathing through the mouth and may suck on ice."**\n\n**Why position is not a comfort measure but a safety one.** Sitting up lowers venous pressure in the head, so the bleeding slows; leaning forward lets blood run **out** of the nose where it can be seen and spat out — hence *"Spit out any blood"*. Lying the patient flat or prone raises nasal venous pressure and sends blood backwards down the pharynx to be **swallowed**, which hides the volume lost, provokes vomiting, and risks aspiration in anyone drowsy. That is the same principle the previous items rest on: swallowed blood is invisible blood (`enthd-nose-282`).\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Head looking forwards** | **correct first aid** — the deck’s *"Head bent forward"* / *"leaning forward"* |\n| **Prone position** | **keyed as the exception** — the patient sits, and never lies face-down |\n| **Nose pinch** | **correct, and the method of choice** — pressure on the **soft cartilaginous** part for **10 minutes**, which compresses Little’s area against the septum. Pinching the bony bridge does nothing |\n| **Decongestant nasal drops** | **correct** — vasoconstriction of the nasal mucosa. `L25.1` puts *"Cotton soaked with decongestant"* first in its anterior-epistaxis sequence, before cautery and packing, and Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-69` prints *decongestant nasal drops* among the accepted treatments of primary epistaxis in children |\n\n**⚠️ The commonest lay error is the opposite of this key**, and it is worth naming: tilting the head **back**. It feels effective because the visible bleeding stops, and it is dangerous for precisely that reason. Grade Gain asks it directly in `entqb-nose2-61`, keying *"Sitting position with the head tilted forward"* against *"Supine position with head tilted back"*.\n\n**Bottom line: sit up, lean forward, pinch the soft part for ten minutes, spit out the blood.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-61`, `entqb-nose2-69`, `entep-nose-89`, and `enthd-nose-280` / `enthd-nose-282` / `enthd-nose-284` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Epistaxis first aid is to sit the patient up leaning forward, pinch the soft cartilaginous nose for ten minutes, spit out swallowed blood and apply a decongestant — never to lie the patient prone or tilt the head back.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.86 (Nose ch.2, Epistaxis). The initial-management list, the ten-minute digital-pressure instruction, *"sitting and leaning forward"*, *"Spit out any blood"* and *"Cotton soaked with decongestant"* are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **Grade Gain prints the position question directly in `entqb-nose2-61` and the decongestant in `entqb-nose2-69` — another bank’s printed keys, preferred to outside reasoning.** The venous-pressure and aspiration reasoning is added and is not on the slide.' }
